WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.339
So far we've looked at the pieces: students, teachers, time, places, and what's taught.

2
00:00:05.339 --> 00:00:08.530
Now we zoom out to the machinery that connects them.

3
00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:12.028
Fair warning: this is the chapter with the most acronyms.

4
00:00:12.028 --> 00:00:13.163
Start at the top.

5
00:00:13.163 --> 00:00:17.906
Under the California Constitution, education is a state responsibility.

6
00:00:17.906 --> 00:00:21.913
Local school districts exist because the state created them.

7
00:00:21.913 --> 00:00:30.329
The state sets graduation requirements, adopts standards, funds most of the budget, and writes the laws districts must follow.

8
00:00:30.329 --> 00:00:33.135
Several players share power in Sacramento.

9
00:00:33.135 --> 00:00:36.675
The Legislature passes education laws and the budget.

10
00:00:36.675 --> 00:00:40.148
The Governor proposes the budget and can veto bills.

11
00:00:40.148 --> 00:00:47.428
The State Board of Education, appointed by the Governor, sets policy on standards, tests, and accountability.

12
00:00:47.428 --> 00:00:55.110
And the State Superintendent of Public Instruction, elected by voters, runs the California Department of Education.

13
00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:58.850
When those players disagree, things can get complicated.

14
00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:03.325
The federal government plays a smaller role than many people think.

15
00:01:03.325 --> 00:01:10.272
Federal dollars make up well under a tenth of school funding in a typical year, closer to one-twentieth.

16
00:01:10.272 --> 00:01:19.623
That share briefly climbed higher during the COVID relief funding of 2020 through 2022, but in normal years it has been sliding for decades.

17
00:01:19.623 --> 00:01:22.027
But those dollars come with strings.

18
00:01:22.027 --> 00:01:36.455
Federal law requires annual testing in certain grades, public reporting of results by student group, extra support for low-income students through what's known as Title I, and services for students with disabilities.

19
00:01:36.455 --> 00:01:40.997
Federal civil rights law also protects students from discrimination.

20
00:01:40.997 --> 00:01:42.800
Small money, big influence.

21
00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:46.747
Most of the day-to-day decisions happen in school districts.

22
00:01:46.747 --> 00:01:57.274
California has about a thousand of them, ranging from Los Angeles Unified, with several hundred thousand students, to tiny rural districts with a single school.

23
00:01:57.274 --> 00:02:08.261
Each is governed by an elected school board, usually five or seven members, who hire a superintendent, approve the budget, negotiate with unions, and set local policy.

24
00:02:08.261 --> 00:02:14.972
School board elections often have low turnout, which means a small number of voters have a lot of say.

25
00:02:14.972 --> 00:02:32.078
Charter schools add another layer: there are well over a thousand of them in California, and each one counts as its own kind of local education agency, operating somewhat outside the traditional district structure even though most are authorized by a district.

26
00:02:32.078 --> 00:02:37.341
Between the state and the districts sit fifty-eight county offices of education.

27
00:02:37.341 --> 00:02:46.091
They review and approve district budgets, which gives them an early-warning role when a district is heading toward financial trouble.

28
00:02:46.091 --> 00:02:56.618
They also run programs that small districts can't manage alone, like certain special education services and schools for students in the juvenile justice system.

29
00:02:56.618 --> 00:02:58.215
Who else shapes policy?

30
00:02:58.215 --> 00:03:01.549
Teachers' unions are among the most influential.

31
00:03:01.549 --> 00:03:09.258
The two big statewide unions are the California Teachers Association and the California Federation of Teachers.

32
00:03:09.258 --> 00:03:18.634
The legal backdrop matters here: the Rodda Act of 1975 established collective bargaining rights for California's teachers, and the U.S.

33
00:03:18.634 --> 00:03:21.481
Supreme Court's 2018 decision in Janus v.

34
00:03:21.481 --> 00:03:30.579
AFSCME ended mandatory "fair share" union fees for public employees, which changed how unions fund themselves and organize members.

35
00:03:30.579 --> 00:03:36.830
Locally, unions bargain contracts covering pay, class size, hours, and working conditions.

36
00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:41.622
At the state level, they're major players in elections and lawmaking.

37
00:03:41.622 --> 00:03:46.900
Supporters see them as the voice of educators and a check on bad management.

38
00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:51.484
Critics say they sometimes block changes that would help students.

39
00:03:51.484 --> 00:03:56.276
Either way, very little happens in California education without them.

40
00:03:56.276 --> 00:04:01.554
California also lets voters make policy directly through ballot initiatives.

41
00:04:01.554 --> 00:04:10.096
Some of the most important education rules in the state, especially around money, came from voters rather than legislators.

42
00:04:10.096 --> 00:04:13.638
We'll see a couple of big ones in the next chapter.

43
00:04:13.638 --> 00:04:33.223
And there are other influencers: parent groups like the PTA, school administrator and school board associations, advocacy organizations, researchers, philanthropists such as the Hewlett Foundation, which has funded education policy work in California for years, and business groups.

44
00:04:33.223 --> 00:04:36.638
How does anyone know if schools are doing their job?

45
00:04:36.638 --> 00:04:38.083
That's accountability.

46
00:04:38.083 --> 00:04:47.213
California's current approach is built around the California School Dashboard, which reports on several measures instead of a single score.

47
00:04:47.213 --> 00:04:49.249
We'll dig into it in Episode 9.

48
00:04:49.249 --> 00:04:56.670
Schools and districts where student groups perform poorly across multiple measures are identified for extra help.

49
00:04:56.670 --> 00:05:05.997
Under state law, that help mostly comes from county offices and a state agency called the California Collaborative for Educational Excellence.

50
00:05:05.997 --> 00:05:18.739
Under federal law, the lowest-performing schools must develop improvement plans, and federal law specifically targets intervention at the bottom five percent of schools statewide by performance.

51
00:05:18.739 --> 00:05:21.629
There's no single fix that works everywhere;

52
00:05:21.629 --> 00:05:27.343
what turns one struggling school around often doesn't work at the school down the road.

53
00:05:27.343 --> 00:05:31.283
California leans toward support rather than harsh penalties.

54
00:05:31.283 --> 00:05:38.048
Finally, a document every parent should know about: the LCAP, or Local Control and Accountability Plan.

55
00:05:38.048 --> 00:05:43.894
Every district, county office, and charter school must write one and update it each year.

56
00:05:43.894 --> 00:05:52.366
It explains the goals, the actions to reach them, and how the money will be spent, especially money meant for high-need students.

57
00:05:52.366 --> 00:06:02.415
By law, districts must involve parents, students, teachers, and the community in developing it, and must hold public hearings before the board adopts it.

58
00:06:02.415 --> 00:06:09.246
If you want to influence your district's priorities, the LCAP process is one of the most direct ways in.

59
00:06:09.246 --> 00:06:13.318
For more, see Chapter 7 of the free Ed100 course at ed100.org.
